{"learned":"We have limited public evidence. Company sources indicate Sam Farber identified a need for ergonomic kitchen tools for users with limited grip strength (supporting T1). No evidence was found for fashion-driven demand (T2 remains unsupported). The census data shows growth in the 65+ population, suggesting a potential market for arthritis-friendly tools, but does not directly link to purchase intent","uncertain":"Whether a sizable segment of consumers will pay a premium for a differentiated ergonomic tool, and whether a fashion-driven segment exists and would pay for style. The two theories predict different drivers of demand.","why_next":"A low‑cost test can directly measure consumer response to two positioning messages (medical necessity vs. fashion) and reveal which driver, if any, generates sufficient purchase intent to justify investment. This will distinguish T1 and T2 within the €20k budget and 12‑week timeline.","call":{"name":"propose_test","arguments":{"name":"Positioning Test: Ergonomic vs. Fashion Messaging","theories":["T1","T2"],"method":"Online survey with randomized exposure to two product descriptions of the same kitchen tool (e.g., a peeler). Participants receive either an ergonomic/medical framing (highlighting ease of use for arthritis/limited grip) or a fashion/style framing (highlighting design, color, and home‑decor appeal). After exposure, participants report their willingness to pay a premium price (e.g., €25 vs. €15) and intent to purchase","sample":"300 U.S. adults recruited via an online panel, stratified to include 100 participants reporting arthritis or limited hand strength (per self‑report) and 100 participants reporting high interest in home décor/style (per self‑report). The 100",
"measure":"Average willingness to pay (WTP) for the tool under each framing; and purchase intent score (1\u201110).","prediction_by_theory":{"T1":"Participants exposed to the ergonomic framing will show a WTP at least \u20ac5 higher than those exposed to the fashion framing, and higher purchase intent among the arthritis/limited\u2011grip subgroup.","T2":"Participants exposed to the fashion framing will show a WTP at least \u20ac5 higher than those exposed to the ergonomic framing, and higher purchase intent among the style\u2011conscious subgroup."},"decision_rule":"If the ergonomic framing yields a statistically significant (p<0.05) WTP premium of \u20ac5 or more over the fashion framing among the arthritis/limited\u2011grip subgroup, proceed with a differentiated ergonomic proposition. If the fashion framing",
